---
// ===========================================================================
// TypeAndInstance.astro — the two lanes, which is the whole page in one look.
//
// Readers reach for a prop or a context to carry the type, because that is how
// everything else works. The point is that the type takes a different road
// entirely: the instance travels at run time through React, and the type
// travels at compile time through the compilation. Drawing them as two lanes
// says it faster than the paragraphs it replaces.
//
// THE TWO LANES ARE DRAWN IDENTICALLY, ON PURPOSE. They were two stacks of
// filled cards in two different fills — the left in a container role, the
// right's last box in another — so the eye read two unrelated components and
// had to be told by the caption that they were a pair. Now every box in both
// lanes is the same outline at the same weight, mirrored about the same
// centres, and the only things drawn as surfaces are the one adapter they both
// come from and the one call they both arrive at. What differs between the
// lanes is what they SAY, which is the diagram's entire argument.
//
// THE COLOURS ARE WRITTEN AS var(--wp-…) RATHER THAN AS UTILITIES because
// `fill` and `stroke` are presentation attributes and there is no class to put
// them in here; they are the same tokens styles.css declares, not new values.
// The faces are utilities, since those exist: mono for what is addressed —
// a call, a declaration, a path — and the serif for what is argued about it.
// ===========================================================================
---

<figure class="mt-[var(--gap-block)]">
  <!-- Scrolls rather than shrinks. Scaled to a 375px column the labels come
       out around five pixels tall, which is a diagram nobody can read; the
       minimum width keeps them legible and the page still never scrolls. -->
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <svg
      viewBox="0 0 720 300"
      role="img"
      aria-labelledby="lanes-title lanes-desc"
      class="w-full min-w-[620px]"
    >
      <title id="lanes-title">The instance and the type take different roads</title>
      <desc id="lanes-desc">
        One adapter feeds two lanes. At run time the form instance travels down
        the React tree through a provider and a component receives it by being
        inside one. At compile time the same adapter is registered by a module
        augmentation, which belongs to the whole compilation, so a component is
        type-checked without importing or receiving anything.
      </desc>

      <g class="font-sans" fill="var(--wp-ink)">
        <!-- The source, and at the bottom the destination: the only two boxes
             cut into the page rather than outlined on it, because they are the
             two things both lanes share. -->
        <rect x="240" y="8" width="240" height="40" rx="3"
          fill="var(--wp-sunk)" stroke="var(--wp-line)" />
        <text x="360" y="26" font-size="12" font-weight="500" text-anchor="middle"
          class="font-mono">zodFormResolver(orderSchema)</text>
        <text x="360" y="41" font-size="10.5" text-anchor="middle"
          fill="var(--wp-ink-quiet)">one adapter</text>

        <path d="M 300 48 L 300 62 L 150 62 L 150 84" fill="none"
          stroke="var(--wp-line)" stroke-width="1" />
        <path d="M 420 48 L 420 62 L 570 62 L 570 84" fill="none"
          stroke="var(--wp-line)" stroke-width="1" />

        <!-- lane headings -->
        <text x="150" y="80" font-size="10" font-weight="500" text-anchor="middle"
          class="font-mono" fill="var(--wp-ink-quiet)">AT RUN TIME</text>
        <text x="570" y="80" font-size="10" font-weight="500" text-anchor="middle"
          class="font-mono" fill="var(--wp-ink-quiet)">AT COMPILE TIME</text>

        <!-- Left lane: the instance. The boxes are wider than they were and
             both lanes grew by the same amount about their own centre line, so
             every connector below still meets them at 150 and at 570. -->
        <rect x="4" y="88" width="292" height="52" rx="3"
          fill="none" stroke="var(--wp-line-faint)" />
        <text x="18" y="110" font-size="11.5" font-weight="500"
          class="font-mono">createForm(adapter)</text>
        <text x="18" y="128" font-size="10.5" fill="var(--wp-ink-quiet)">the cell space — one per form on screen</text>

        <line x1="150" y1="140" x2="150" y2="162" stroke="var(--wp-line)" stroke-width="1" />

        <rect x="4" y="166" width="292" height="52" rx="3"
          fill="none" stroke="var(--wp-line-faint)" />
        <text x="18" y="188" font-size="11.5" font-weight="500"
          class="font-mono">&lt;FormProvider form={"{form}"}&gt;</text>
        <text x="18" y="206" font-size="10.5" fill="var(--wp-ink-quiet)">travels DOWN the React tree</text>

        <!-- right lane: the type -->
        <rect x="424" y="88" width="292" height="52" rx="3"
          fill="none" stroke="var(--wp-line-faint)" />
        <text x="438" y="110" font-size="11.5" font-weight="500"
          class="font-mono">declare module "@maroonedog/waypoint"</text>
        <text x="438" y="128" font-size="10.5" fill="var(--wp-ink-quiet)">WaypointForms — one file, one time</text>

        <line x1="570" y1="140" x2="570" y2="162" stroke="var(--wp-line)" stroke-width="1" />

        <rect x="424" y="166" width="292" height="52" rx="3"
          fill="none" stroke="var(--wp-line-faint)" />
        <!-- Serif, where its opposite number on the left is mono: this box is
             the one claim in the diagram that names nothing you could type. -->
        <text x="438" y="188" font-size="12" font-weight="600">the whole COMPILATION</text>
        <text x="438" y="206" font-size="10.5" fill="var(--wp-ink-quiet)">not the import graph — nothing is imported</text>

        <!-- they meet -->
        <path d="M 150 218 L 150 238 L 360 238 L 360 252" fill="none"
          stroke="var(--wp-line)" stroke-width="1" />
        <path d="M 570 218 L 570 238 L 360 238" fill="none"
          stroke="var(--wp-line)" stroke-width="1" />

        <rect x="196" y="252" width="328" height="40" rx="3"
          fill="var(--wp-sunk)" stroke="var(--wp-line)" />
        <!-- The one accent in the diagram, on the one character the addressing
             scheme turns on. It is the same three ranks a code block's label
             is set in, and nothing else here is allowed to wear the mark. -->
        <text x="360" y="270" font-size="12.5" font-weight="500" text-anchor="middle"
          class="font-mono"
          >useField("form<tspan fill="var(--wp-mark)">:</tspan>billing.postcode")</text>
        <text x="360" y="285" font-size="10.5" text-anchor="middle"
          fill="var(--wp-ink-quiet)">no import, no prop, and still checked</text>
      </g>
    </svg>
  </div>
  <figcaption class="caption">
    The right-hand lane is the unusual one. A module augmentation belongs to the
    compilation, so a component ten levels down is checked by a file it has
    never heard of — and a registration that falls out of <code>tsconfig</code>'s{" "}
    <code>include</code> fails loudly rather than degrading back to{" "}
    <code>string</code>.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
